  "paper": "TECH3",
  "unit": "7",
  "sub": "Cost types: direct, indirect, fixed, variable, sunk, opportunity",
  "q": "Expenses incurred by a firm on recruitment, training & development of employees are treated as",
  "opts": {
   "A": "Current costs",
   "B": "Incremental costs",
   "C": "Replacement costs",
   "D": "Implicit costs"
  },
  "ans": "A",
  "exp": "In Human Resource Accounting, the 'Replacement Cost' approach values an employee by the cost a firm would incur to replace them with someone of equivalent competence — and that cost is explicitly built up from recruitment, selection and training/development expenses, matching the question almost word-for-word. 'Implicit costs' (D) is wrong because these are real cash outlays, not opportunity costs of self-owned resources; 'Current costs' (A) and 'Incremental costs' (B) are generic accounting labels that do not specifically capture the HR-accounting concept the question is testing. This question is genuinely ambiguous across accounting frameworks and the printed key may differ.",
  "prov": "Official MPSC final answer key",
